title op6: the claimed guard again, with the sort order flipped

# op6-claimed-guard's twin, and the reason it is a PAIR rather than a
# fixture.  Read them together or neither says what it means.
#
# THE NAMES HERE ARE LOAD BEARING.  The realization walk assigns
# output pools ids in ascending first-name order and walks them in
# that order (source-verified by the kernel lane).  So whether a
# one-pass loop gets the right answer depends entirely on which pool
# sorts first, and the two fixtures put the same shape on either side
# of that line:
#
#   op6-claimed-guard   P2 /aa (lost-set route) sorts BEFORE
#                       P1 /y  (surviving name).  A one-pass loop
#                       lets P2 take the dnode first, and P1 -- the
#                       pool with the better claim -- materializes.
#                       WRONG.
#
#   this fixture        P1 /a  (surviving name) sorts BEFORE
#                       P2 /zz (lost-set route).  A one-pass loop
#                       reaches P1 first, so it claims the dnode and
#                       P2 materializes.  RIGHT, and right BY LUCK:
#                       sort order happened to agree with precedence.
#
# The shared gold is the invariant: THE DNODE GOES TO THE POOL
# HOLDING THE SURVIVING NAME, in both fixtures, whichever way the
# names sort.  Two passes satisfy that on both.  One pass satisfies
# it on exactly one, and the one it satisfies is the one where the
# accident falls its way.
#
# A single fixture cannot state this.  "The answer does not depend on
# sort order" is an independence claim, and independence needs at
# least two points -- one fixture is a point, and a point is
# consistent with any slope.  op6-claimed-guard alone would pass
# under a one-pass implementation the moment somebody tidied /aa and
# /y into friendlier names, and it would still look like it was
# testing the guard.
#
# So: if you rename anything in either file, rename it in both, keep
# the sort relation, and check that the two fixtures still disagree
# about which one a one-pass loop gets right.  That disagreement is
# the test.
#
# THE SHAPE, identical to its twin.  Base holds one file under two
# names, a and x.  Onto does nothing.  Off-of un-shares them: a stays
# on the original dnode, and x becomes a separate object renamed to
# zz.  Two output pools want onto's dnode 10-100 -- P1 because a is a
# name onto still holds, P2 because its lost name x was on it -- and
# they cannot both have it.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none; no directory changed, /shared is green
#   deltas:          P2 lost {x}, gained {zz}.  P1 lost nothing; a
#                    survived, so it is in no delta
#   decisions:       P1 {a} reuses onto's 10-100 by name-driven
#                    lookup in pass one.  P2 {zz} materializes: pass
#                    two's lost-set route reaches 10-100 and the
#                    claimed guard refuses it
#   conflicts:       none.  Onto is quiet; this is purely a
#                    realization question
#   emission:        materialize zz, link it, unlink x

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: a = v0
      10-100: x

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: a = v0
      10-100: x

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: a = v0
      40-300: zz = v0

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared
      10-100: a = v0
      40-300: zz = v0
